{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Or a hunchback.</b> שׁורי\"ציולש בְּלַעַז — whose eyelashes are long and lie down." "<b>Or a diseased eye.</b> Who has a דֹּק in his eye, which they call טיל\"א, as in ״הַנּוֹטֶה כַדֹּק״ (Isaiah 40)." "<b>Or a blemished eye.</b> A thing that confuses the eye — such as a white thread extending from the white and cutting into the iris, which is the ring surrounding the black, called פרוני\"לא, and this thread cuts through the ring and enters the black. And the Targum renders תְבַלּוּל חִלִּיז — a term of חִלָּזוֹן, for it resembles a worm, that thread; and so the Sages of Israel called it in the blemishes of the firstborn: חִלָּזוֹן, נָחָשׁ, עֵינָב (Bechorot 38)." "<b>Scurvy or yalephet.</b> These are kinds of sores. גָּרָב is חֶרֶס — a sore dry inside and outside; יַלֶּפֶת is Egyptian חֲזָזִית. And why is it called יַלֶּפֶת? Because it מְלַלְפֶּפֶת and persists until the day of death, and it is moist outside and dry inside. And elsewhere he calls גָּרָב a sore moist outside and dry inside, as it says ״וּבַגָּרָב וּבֶחָרֶס״ (Deuteronomy 28) — when גָּרָב is placed next to חֶרֶס, he calls יַלֶּפֶת גָּרָב; and when it is placed next to יַלֶּפֶת, he calls חֶרֶס גָּרָב. Thus is explicitly in Bechorot (41a)." "<b>Crushed testicles.</b> According to the Targum: מְרַס פַּחֲדִין — that his פַּחֲדִין are crushed — his testicles are pounded. פַּחֲדִין as in ״גִּידֵי פַּחֲדָיו יְשֹׂרָגוּ״ (Job 40)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French sorcilles, 'eyebrows/eyelashes'; the term refers to abnormally drooping eyelashes", 1 "the la'az טיל\"א is Old French tele, 'web/film' over the eye", 2 "the la'az פרוני\"לא is Old French prunelle, 'pupil/iris'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:43.735388011Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}